ied to  make it as comfortable as possible says  this doctor who works nearby  it's not the first time these bunkers  have been converted to bomb shelters  when nazi germany invaded the soviet  union in 1941 odessa was one of its  first targets  the city fought back for 73 days and  inflicted significant casualties before  finally falling to the nazis  for their bravery soviet leader joseph  stalin later proclaimed odessa hero city  that name is still written on the main  train station where thousands are now  fleeing war once again  images from the city in 1941 look eerily  similar to today  history repeats itself this local  historian says but this time it's the  russian nazis doing the attacking he  shows us letters and diaries from the  second world war written by ukrainian  soldiers and their families  now this historian is writing his own  war diary  the situation is horrible my wife and  children have left the country he says  i'm ready to pick up a gun and fight  this ukrainian canadian was just three  years old in 1942 his earliest childhood  memory is hiding in a bunker and hearing  the bombs all the lights went off it was  a  terrible thud that bomb had just fallen  near the  bunker his family eventually escaped  ukraine and fled to canada after  odessa's liberation in 1944 the city of  vancouver officially proclaimed odessa  its first sister city and sent  desperately needed humanitarian aid  they're very similar vancouver has a  port odessa has support  vancouver is vibrant odessa is very  vibrant on tuesday the mayors of  vancouver and odessa spoke by phone the  canadian city once again offering its  support in a time of war vancouver city  hall lighting up in ukrainian blue and  yellow but unlike during the second  world war this time western countries  are not mobilizing their militaries to  help  it's just disastrous it's a slaughter of  ukrainian people and  devastation of ukraine  jeff sample global news odessa  well just ahead the pickup that got  picked up as tornadoes roared across  texas  well check out the heart-pounding moment  a pickup truck was swept up in a tornado  it flipped and went spinning but then  look at this it lands back on its wheels  and manages to drive away  all that flying debris is from a nearby  mobile home more than 20 tornadoes left  a trail of destruction across north and  central texas and oklahoma overnight  causing extensive damage to homes and  schools one person is dead and many  others are injured  rainfall from that same storm also hit  other parts of texas where it offered a  reprieve from a slew of wildfires  more than 170 fires had burned across  the state over the past week torching  dozens of buildings firefighters are  making progress with the largest fires  near fort worth 60 contained though  heavy winds continue to complicate their  efforts in drier areas  well next the struggles ahead for  millions of civilians who have fled the  war in ukraine  the ukrainian refugee crisis is about to  surpass worst case predictions nearly 4  million people have fled and thousands  still cross into neighboring countries  every single day  the sheer numbers are just one of the  challenges for governments across europe  as mike armstrong reports from poland  those fleeing can't simply leave behind  the trauma of war  there is a difference between the waves  of refugees flowing out of ukraine now  and the ones that left early on there  isn't just the trauma of leaving their  homes that would be enough but for those  leaving in what is week four many  toughed it out and now know what it  means to live in cities under siege  this woman says between the bombings and  not knowing how it'll end it was too  scary to stay she took her child but  left her husband and mother behind  the world health organization is warning  many people fleeing ukraine need more  than just food and shelter there are  basic health issues many arrive at  border crossings after traveling for  days without proper food or water there  are also problems caused by distress the  who representative for poland says in  this country about 30 000 refugees have  severe mental diseases but there's still  a much bigger number dealing with trauma  with the number of refugees now about  half a million require support for  mental health disorders due to the  conflict  the challenges for a country like poland  are massive the vast majority of  refugees are women and children  shelter's just one problem among many  the minister of education says the  country's school system is adding about  10 000 students a day the u.n predicted  and planned for up to four million  refugees leaving ukraine they're likely  to blow by that number by the weekend we  don't plan to put out a new planning  figure  but of course we're ready to react  now the numbers crossing each day have  dropped this polish train station near  the border was packed a week ago things  have slowed down but the mayor says  they're still getting about 8 000  refugees going through his city each day  and helping them through he calls a  marathon after one month and a lot other  people is very tired so we have to think  about next months or maybe hopefully not  but maybe  about years  that lack of a timeline is one of the  issues for refugees as well they're  living a marathon and have no way of  knowing where or when it ends  mike armstrong global news cheshire  poland  and that's global national for this  tuesday night i'm jackson prosko as the  war continues life goes on in ukraine  and in some cases it begins we leave you  with glimpses of that tonight the city  of kiev shared these photos monday  showing newborns with their mothers at a  maternity ward and since the invasion  began city officials say 807 new  children have been born in the capital  thanks for watching we hope to see you  again tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

